---
import { WA_CHROME, WHATSAPP_DISPLAY, waHref } from '../data/whatsapp.js';
// VISUAILS — root layout. Ports the SvelteKit v2 +layout.svelte chrome
// (header/nav, mobile nav, footer, grain overlay, conversion bar) to Astro.
// <ClientRouter /> turns this into an SPA-like app with native browser
// View Transitions between pages — see src/scripts/interactions.js for the
// astro:page-load re-init hook that keeps reveal/magnetic/etc. working
// after a transition.
import { ClientRouter } from 'astro:transitions';
import '../styles/global.css';
// Self-hosted Archivo (Fontsource) — served from our own build instead of
// Google's servers (AVG/GDPR-clean, no third-party connection). font-display
// is 'swap' by default in Fontsource, matching the old &display=swap.
//
// ONE VARIABLE FILE, NOT EIGHT STATIC CUTS. This used to import 400 / 500 /
// 600 / 700 / 800 / 900 / 400-italic / 700-italic — eight woff2 requests per
// subset, on the critical path, against a budget of "LCP under 2.5s on mobile
// 4G with the shader running". Measured on a 1.6Mbps / 150ms / 4x-CPU mobile
// profile, /catalog's LCP element was its own H1 at 3372ms: the page was
// waiting on fonts, not on the shader. The weight axis covers 100–900 in one
// file, so every weight the site already uses (including the 500s, 600s and
// 800s scattered through the components) keeps rendering — DESIGN.md called
// for exactly this swap and it had not been made.
//
// The wght axis, not the wdth build: nothing in the system varies width, and
// the wdth file is a second axis nobody reads.
import '@fontsource-variable/archivo/wght.css';
import '@fontsource-variable/archivo/wght-italic.css';
// The latin roman is the only font file on the critical path — it sets the H1
// that is the LCP element on most pages. Measured: /catalog's LCP was 2840ms
// on the mobile profile purely waiting for it to arrive, because a woff2
// referenced from inside a stylesheet is not discoverable until that
// stylesheet has been fetched and parsed. Preloading it moves the request to
// the first byte of the document. Only this one file — preloading a font the
// page may never use costs the same bandwidth as one it does.
import archivoRoman from '@fontsource-variable/archivo/files/archivo-latin-wght-normal.woff2?url';
// Bodoni Moda italic (task #271b) — same self-hosting reason as Archivo
// above: AVG/GDPR-clean, no third-party font request. Two weights only —
// 500 for quote-weight prose, 700 for the heavier heading-emphasis word —
// because the whole point of this accent is that it appears in a few
// specific places (see global.css's --font-editorial comment), not as a
// general-purpose family with a full weight range to pick from.
// ── THE HEADING FACE AND THE ACCENT FACE, August 2026 ───────────────────────
//
// Bodoni Moda came out and these two went in. Lucas: "kijk ook naar betere
// fonts want ik mis hier wat aesthetic, en pas de visualized-font aan naar iets
// serieuzer en strakker en intenser." Bodoni's swash italic was the fashion-
// magazine reflex — the same lane PRODUCT.md's anti-references reject — and one
// grotesque doing both headings and body was why the page read as unvoiced.
//
// Big Shoulders Display carries h1/h2 only. It is condensed industrial signage
// lettering: tall, narrow, engineered, which is the "instrument, not a service
// page" the brand personality asks for, and narrow enough that a long Dutch
// heading stops overflowing on a phone instead of starting to.
//
// Anybody italic carries one word. A technical grotesque with a real italic —
// so the accent word is emphasis in an industrial voice rather than a guest
// from another magazine. Both are self-hosted for the same AVG reason as
// Archivo: no third-party font request from a customer's browser.
//
// Both are the wght axis only. Neither varies width here, and a wdth build is a
// second axis nobody reads.
// No .css on the specifier: this package's exports map has only "./*" ->
// "./*.css", so "/wght.css" resolves to "wght.css.css" and the build fails.
// Anybody ships an extra "./*.css" entry, which is why the line below keeps it.
import '@fontsource-variable/big-shoulders-display/wght';
import '@fontsource-variable/anybody/wght-italic.css';
import bigShoulders from '@fontsource-variable/big-shoulders-display/files/big-shoulders-display-latin-wght-normal.woff2?url';
import { ui, brandModelHref, ogLocale, localeNames, getLangFromPath, stripLang, localizedPath, useTranslations } from '../i18n/ui.js';
import { graphJson } from '../data/schema.js';
import CookieConsent from '../components/CookieConsent.astro';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  ogImage?: string;
  noindex?: boolean;
  /** Opt this page onto the espresso ground (section 17's rebuild). Per-page
   *  rather than global on purpose: 60-odd pages were composed for a light
   *  ground and go dark with no other change, which is a regression that
   *  happens to be intentional. Pages move across as they are rebuilt; when
   *  the last one is over, .ground-espresso's contents become :root and this
   *  prop is deleted. The migration lives in the markup, not in a head. */
  ground?: 'paper' | 'espresso';
}

// noindex is for the pages that exist to be arrived at, not found. Two use it:
// /thank-you and /nl/thank-you. Both were shipping `index, follow` while being
// absent from sitemap.xml — a contradiction search engines resolve in favour of
// the meta tag, which is the wrong way round. A thank-you page in the results
// costs a real conversion every time someone lands on it cold.
//
// The order-status lookup used to be the third. It is gone: /order-status is a
// 301 to the client portal at /o, which is a Pages Function and never renders
// through this layout. The portal sets its own, stricter headers —
// `noindex, nofollow, noarchive` as a meta tag AND an x-robots-tag response
// header, because a portal URL contains a token and a meta tag alone is only
// read by something that parses HTML. See src/lib/portal.js.
const { title, description, ogImage = '/img/banners-05.webp', noindex = false, ground = 'paper' } = Astro.props;

// Privacy-first analytics — Cloudflare Web Analytics, which is cookieless and
// sets nothing on the visitor's device.
//
// TURNING IT ON IS A BUILD SETTING, NOT A CODE EDIT. Set
// PUBLIC_CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN in the Pages project (Settings → Variables and
// Secrets → **plaintext Variable**, not a Secret) and redeploy. The token is
// public by design — it ends up in the page source of every site that uses it —
// so it is not a credential and a Secret would only make it harder to read
// back. The empty default means an unconfigured build ships with analytics
// simply absent.
//
// This used to be a literal on this line, which meant switching analytics on
// required editing a source file and pushing a commit. That is fine once and
// annoying forever, and it puts a value that belongs to the deployment inside
// the thing being deployed.
//
// NOTE THE PREVIOUS COMMENT WAS WRONG about consent: it said cookieless
// analytics "needs no consent banner". Cookieless means no cookie-storage
// consent under ePrivacy, which is not the same as no consent at all, and the
// safe side of that argument is cheap. The beacon is gated on the banner —
// see src/scripts/consent.js — and does not load until someone says yes.
//
// Custom events (CTA clicks, "how did you find us?") are wired via track() in
// interactions.js and start reporting the moment a tool that supports them
// (e.g. Plausible/Umami) is added.
const CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN ?? '';

// Language is derived from the URL: /nl/... → Dutch, else English.
const path = Astro.url.pathname;
const lang = getLangFromPath(path);
const t = useTranslations(lang);
// The two WhatsApp prefills in this file — the floating launcher and the
// drawer's chat button — were hardcoded English on all 86 pages, so a Dutch
// visitor's chat opened in a language they had not chosen, and the launcher's
// one still said "a drop", the word the pricing rewrite retired. Every other
// WhatsApp link on the site was already per-locale; these two were simply
// missed. A const with exactly this fix already sat here, unused, because the
// hrefs below were never wired to it — which is why the bug survived a round
// of "fixed it". The strings now live in src/data/whatsapp.js with the number,
// and the wiring is the only thing in this file.
const wa = WA_CHROME[lang] || WA_CHROME.en;
const lp = (p: string) => localizedPath(lang, p);   // localize a base path for this language
const base = stripLang(path);                        // language-neutral path (for switcher + hreflang)

/* Elke ingang draagt zijn eigen href sinds 9 augustus 2026 — zie de noot bij
   NAV_SOON in src/i18n/ui.js. Hier stond een map() die drie parallelle lijsten
   op index aan elkaar reeg, en die kon stil verschuiven. */
const drops = (ui[lang] || ui.en).drops;
/* De tweede laag — zie de noot bij `how` in src/i18n/ui.js. Dezelfde vorm als
   `drops`, dus dezelfde renderlus kan hem tekenen. */
const how = (ui[lang] || ui.en).how || [];

// ── The signed-in chrome's own copy ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Everything else in this header comes from t() / src/i18n/ui.js, and these
// three strings belong there too. They are HERE because ui.js was outside the
// set of files this task was allowed to edit — that is the whole reason, and it
// is not a pattern to copy. The next person free to touch ui.js should move
// ACCOUNT_UI into it as nav_signout / nav_account_label / nav_account_aria and
// delete this block; nothing else reads it.
//
// nav_account itself ('Log in' / 'Inloggen') stays in ui.js and stays the
// SIGNED-OUT text, because that is what a static build has to ship — see the
// script at the foot of this file.
const ACCOUNT_UI = {
  en: {
    signout: 'Sign out',
    // Screen-reader name for the link that becomes the brand's own name. On
    // its own "VISUAILS" in a nav bar does not say it is an account link.
    aria: 'Your account',
  },
  nl: {
    signout: 'Uitloggen',
    aria: 'Je account',
  },
};
const acct = ACCOUNT_UI[lang === 'nl' ? 'nl' : 'en'];

const isActive = (href: string) => (href === '/' ? base === '/' : base === href || base.startsWith(href + '/'));
const year = new Date().getFullYear();

// SEO — absolute canonical + OG image built off the configured site origin.
const SITE = 'https://visuails.com';
const canonical = new URL(lp(base), Astro.site ?? SITE).href;
const ogImageAbs = new URL(ogImage, Astro.site ?? SITE).href;

// hreflang alternates — the same page in each language, x-default → English.
const alternates = [
  { hreflang: 'en', href: new URL(base, Astro.site ?? SITE).href },
  { hreflang: 'nl', href: new URL(localizedPath('nl', base), Astro.site ?? SITE).href },
  { hreflang: 'x-default', href: new URL(base, Astro.site ?? SITE).href },
];
const switchLangs = ['en', 'nl'];

// The language switcher used to carry two 15x11 inline-SVG national flags.
// They were the last saturated colour in the site chrome — a Union Jack and a
// Dutch tricolour beside an ink-and-paper interface where the photography is
// meant to be the only colour on screen. The switcher already renders the
// locale abbreviation (EN / NL from localeNames), which is unambiguous, needs
// no flag-to-language mapping, and does not equate a language with a country.

// Structured data. One hand-written ProfessionalService node used to sit here
// and go out on all 68 pages. Section 3 of the brief forbids LocalBusiness
// schema and ProfessionalService is a LocalBusiness subtype, so the node is
// gone — along with its `priceRange`, which is a LocalBusiness property and
// which the real Offers replace with exact figures. The graph is now built per
// route in src/data/schema.js: Organization everywhere, Service on the pillar
// pages, Product/Offer on the priced pages, FAQPage where the questions are.
// See that file for the rules it is held to — no invented facts, no delivery
// promise, no typed number.
//
// It takes the canonical URL rather than rebuilding one, so every @id in the
// graph matches the <link rel="canonical"> below it exactly.
const jsonLd = graphJson({ path: base, lang, url: canonical });
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang={lang}>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" href={archivoRoman} crossorigin="anonymous" />
  <!-- The heading face IS the LCP element on most pages — same measured
       reasoning as the Archivo preload above. Anybody is deliberately NOT
       preloaded: it draws one word, below the fold on every page but the
       homepage, and a preload for a font the page may never paint costs the
       same bandwidth as one it does. -->
  <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" href={bigShoulders} crossorigin="anonymous" />
  {/* Favicons. All five files are rendered from the SAME traced path as
      <symbol id="markglyph"> further down this file — the V mark in white on
      the page ground, so the tab icon and the mark in the header cannot drift
      apart. The generator is scripts/make-favicons.mjs; re-run it after any
      change to the glyph or to --bg-0.

      White on a #08090B square, NOT white on transparent: a transparent white
      mark is invisible on Chrome and Safari's light tab strip, which is the
      default most people are looking at.

      Order matters. A browser takes the LAST icon link it understands, so the
      .ico goes first as the floor and the SVG last as the ceiling: anything
      modern renders the vector and stays crisp at 16px on a scaled display,
      anything old falls back down the list. */}
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="32x32" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="/favicon-32.png" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="192x192" href="/favicon-192.png" />
  <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
  {/* Full-bleed square, no rounded corners: iOS applies its own mask and does
      not honour transparency, so a pre-rounded tile gets rounded twice and
      shows dark slivers in the corners. */}
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
  <title>{title}</title>
  <meta name="description" content={description} />
  <link rel="canonical" href={canonical} />
  {alternates.map((a) => <link rel="alternate" hreflang={a.hreflang} href={a.href} />)}
  <meta name="robots" content={noindex ? 'noindex, follow' : 'index, follow, max-image-preview:large'} />
  {/* --bg-0, by hand. verify2 §6 allowlists `theme-color`, so this is the one
      palette value in the codebase that no check can see drift — it is listed
      in DESIGN.md's swap checklist for that reason and nowhere else. It was
      still the retired paper value (#F3F5F8) after section 18, which painted a
      near-white address bar above a near-black page on Android Chrome. */}
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#08090B" />
  <meta property="og:title" content={title} />
  <meta property="og:description" content={description} />
  <meta property="og:image" content={ogImageAbs} />
  <meta property="og:url" content={canonical} />
  <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="VISUAILS" />
  <meta property="og:locale" content={ogLocale[lang]} />
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
  <meta name="twitter:title" content={title} />
  <meta name="twitter:description" content={description} />
  <meta name="twitter:image" content={ogImageAbs} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={jsonLd} />
  <!-- The reveal gate.

       203 elements across this site ship `class="reveal pending"` from the
       server, and global.css only hides them when <html> carries `js`. This
       is the script that puts it there, and the only reason it is inline and
       in <head> is timing: it has to run before first paint or the page
       flashes its content and then hides it.

       There used to be a <noscript> override undoing the hiding instead. That
       covered JS being switched off. It did nothing about the likelier
       failure — JS on, bundle never arrives — where <noscript> is inert and
       the visitor gets a fully styled page with no content in it. Hence the
       last two lines: interactions.js sets __vRevealLive when its reveal net
       is bound, and if that has not happened shortly after load, the gate
       comes off and the page is simply visible with no animation. A reveal
       that fails should degrade to "no animation", never to "no page".

       astro:after-swap is not optional. Astro's ClientRouter removes every
       attribute from <html> on a soft navigation and copies the incoming
       document's attributes over — class included — so without that line the
       reveals would work on the first page and silently stop on every page
       after it. -->
  <script is:inline>
    (function () {
      if (window.__vRevealGate) return;
      window.__vRevealGate = 1;
      var root = document.documentElement;
      var abandoned = false;
      // Someone who asked for reduced motion should never see the hidden
      // state at all — not even for the moment between this script and the
      // module that uncovers it. Read per call, not cached: the setting can
      // change mid-session and this runs again on every soft navigation.
      function motionOK() {
        return !(window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches);
      }
      function on() { if (!abandoned && motionOK()) root.classList.add('js'); }
      function check() {
        if (abandoned || window.__vRevealLive) return;
        abandoned = true;
        root.classList.remove('js');
      }
      on();
      document.addEventListener('astro:after-swap', on);
      window.addEventListener('load', function () { setTimeout(check, 600); });
      setTimeout(check, 5000);
    })();
  </script>
  <noscript>
    <style>
      /* The reveals no longer need a line here — with no JS there is no `js`
         class, so global.css never hides them in the first place. What is
         left is the compare slider, whose idle demo is a pure CSS animation
         and so runs happily without the drag handling that gives it a point.
         Pinned at the halfway mark instead: a control that cannot be moved
         should not spend all day advertising that it moves. */
      .cmp .cmp-after { animation: none !important; clip-path: inset(0 0 0 50%) !important; }

      /* Homepage "drop or single product" tabs (task #268) — interactions.js
         is what shows/hides the two tt-panel elements, so with no JS the
         second one would stay server-rendered `hidden` forever and that
         content would simply not exist for a no-JS visitor. Un-hide both and
         disable the now-inert tab buttons rather than leave one path silent. */
      .tt-tab { pointer-events: none; }
      .tt-panel[hidden] { display: grid !important; }
      .tt-panel + .tt-panel { margin-top: 1.4rem; padding-top: 1.4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
    </style>
  </noscript>
  <ClientRouter />
</head>
<body class={ground === "espresso" ? "ground-espresso" : undefined}>
{/* THE FIRST TAB STOP. It has to be the first child of <body>, not merely
    before <main>: placed after the header it is the twelfth stop, which is
    eleven stops too late to be a skip link. Even with the drawer inert, the header is a handful of
      controls in front of every page's content, and a keyboard or screen-reader
      user had no way past them — the site had no in-page anchor at all. Visually
      hidden until focused, which is the only state it needs to exist in. */}
<a class="skip-link" href="#main">{t('nav_skip')}</a>

  <!-- SVG sprite — product-scene placeholder symbols, the chrome gradients and
       the outlined logotype. The six greys below (three gObj stops, two gGlass
       stops, one flat #B9B7B4 used for caps and soles) are cut from the SAME
       ink ramp as the interface, so the placeholder object reads as an unlit
       neutral form rather than a second, competing colour temperature.

       They have been re-cut twice, and the second time is the lesson. Round
       one took them off a blue-grey of their own (#b7c0d1 / #8591a8 / #525d75)
       and onto the then-current ink ramp, which was hue 265. Round two is the
       temperature swap: the interface ramp rotated to hue 75 and these did not
       follow it, so for one revision this comment claimed a shared ramp that
       had stopped being shared — the sprite was the competing temperature the
       sentence above exists to forbid. Nothing enumerated it, because it is a
       hand-carried literal in markup rather than a token. It is row 10 of
       DESIGN.md's "Changing the palette" checklist now, which is the only
       thing that will catch it the third time.

       Every value is the old L and C at hue 75, so the object's modelling is
       untouched — the largest luminance move across all six is +0.0012, which
       is below one 8-bit step. The white fills are specular highlights on the
       object and stay white — that is how a lit surface behaves, not a
       palette leak. -->
  <svg width="0" height="0" style="position:absolute" aria-hidden="true">
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="gObj" x1="0" y1="0" x2="1" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0" stop-color="#B3B1AE"/><stop offset="0.5" stop-color="#82807D"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#4E4D4B"/>
      </linearGradient>
      <linearGradient id="gGlass" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
        <stop offset="0" stop-color="#D5D4D2" stop-opacity="0.97"/><stop offset="1" stop-color="#7C7A77" stop-opacity="0.9"/>
      </linearGradient>
      <!-- Four <linearGradient>s used to sit here: chromeInk / chromePaper and
           their near-square chromeInkMark / chromePaperMark twins, the twelve
           spec stops remapped per ground and solved to 3.25:1 so a 17px word
           did not develop holes in its letterforms. They are gone with the
           chrome logotype, and the two that remain above are not part of that
           family — gObj and gGlass light the placeholder product shapes and
           have nothing to do with the wordmark. If a chrome logotype is ever
           reinstated, chrome_ramp.py still emits all four; it was the ramp's
           single source and was never deleted. -->
    </defs>
    <symbol id="p-bottle" viewBox="0 0 120 150">
      <rect x="50" y="8" width="20" height="16" rx="3" fill="url(#gObj)"/>
      <rect x="46" y="22" width="28" height="10" rx="2" fill="#B9B7B4"/>
      <path d="M42 34 h36 a10 10 0 0 1 10 10 v82 a12 12 0 0 1 -12 12 h-32 a12 12 0 0 1 -12 -12 v-82 a10 10 0 0 1 10 -10 z" fill="url(#gGlass)"/>
      <rect x="46" y="70" width="28" height="46" rx="6" fill="#ffffff" opacity="0.18"/>
      <rect x="50" y="46" width="7" height="70" rx="3" fill="#ffffff" opacity="0.5"/>
    </symbol>
    <symbol id="p-sneaker" viewBox="0 0 160 110">
      <path d="M14 78 q0 -30 26 -34 q10 -2 20 6 l26 20 q16 4 46 8 q14 2 14 14 v6 q0 6 -8 6 H20 q-8 0 -8 -8 z" fill="url(#gGlass)"/>
      <path d="M40 46 q10 -4 20 4 l24 18" fill="none" stroke="#ffffff" stroke-opacity="0.55" stroke-width="3"/>
      <path d="M14 92 h132 q0 10 -10 10 H22 q-8 0 -8 -10z" fill="#B9B7B4"/>
      <circle cx="52" cy="60" r="3" fill="#fff" opacity="0.7"/><circle cx="66" cy="68" r="3" fill="#fff" opacity="0.7"/>
    </symbol>
    <symbol id="p-jar" viewBox="0 0 120 130">
      <ellipse cx="60" cy="30" rx="34" ry="12" fill="#B9B7B4"/>
      <rect x="26" y="30" width="68" height="16" fill="#B9B7B4"/>
      <path d="M22 52 h76 v40 a20 20 0 0 1 -20 20 h-36 a20 20 0 0 1 -20 -20 z" fill="url(#gGlass)"/>
      <ellipse cx="60" cy="52" rx="38" ry="12" fill="url(#gObj)"/>
      <ellipse cx="60" cy="50" rx="24" ry="7" fill="#fff" opacity="0.35"/>
    </symbol>
    <symbol id="p-bag" viewBox="0 0 120 130">
      <path d="M40 40 a20 20 0 0 1 40 0" fill="none" stroke="url(#gObj)" stroke-width="6"/>
      <path d="M26 44 h68 l8 74 a6 6 0 0 1 -6 7 H24 a6 6 0 0 1 -6 -7 z" fill="url(#gGlass)"/>
      <rect x="26" y="44" width="68" height="8" fill="#ffffff" opacity="0.25"/>
      <circle cx="60" cy="82" r="7" fill="#fff" opacity="0.4"/>
    </symbol>
    <!-- The two brand marks, outlined.

         Source: the two PNGs Lucas supplied (the V glyph and the VISUAILS
         logotype). Both arrived as flat two-colour rasters on a transparent
         ground, which a raster cannot survive here for two reasons: a fixed
         ink colour goes invisible the first time the palette moves (the
         previous wordmark raster was #EEF0F6, which measured 1.05:1 the day
         the ground turned pale — invisible in a way no contrast checker
         reports, because a checker looks at text and this is an image), and a
         raster cannot take a gradient or a scope-inherited fill at all.
         So each PNG was thresholded, cropped to its ink bounding box, and
         traced to a single path. The logotype's ink occupied 114,270→1759,543
         of its source, hence the 1650×278 viewBox. Cropping to the ink is what
         makes `height:` alone a reliable sizing lever — there is no invisible
         padding baked in.

         THE V GLYPH WAS REDRAWN IN AUGUST 2026 and is on its second trace. The
         old one was a V with a small leaf to its upper right, 642×658. Lucas
         supplied a new drawing — the same V with a sharp spike where the leaf
         was — and it is better in the one place a mark does most of its work:
         at 16px the leaf collapsed into a blob of pixels and the spike stays a
         spike. (A first attempt at the same idea was rejected on the same
         test; rendered at 16 and 24px it read as a W, not a V. The lesson is
         that a mark is judged at the tab strip, not on the artboard.)

         It is on its THIRD drawing and its second rebuild. Lucas supplied a
         final, higher-resolution version once the shape was settled; its ink
         occupies 264,201→965,1014 of a 1254px source, so 702×813 after
         cropping — TALLER than wide, where the original mark was square. That
         difference is why the two <use> sites below carry the viewBox too;
         they hardcode it, and left on a stale one the glyph renders cropped
         and off-centre with nothing failing.

         IT IS NOT A TRACE. This path was rebuilt as geometry, and the reason is
         that a trace of this drawing is visibly wrong when you look closely:
         potrace follows the anti-aliased edge of a 647px raster, so every one
         of these dead-straight edges came out as a chain of ~1400 coordinates
         with a fine ripple along it, plus a two-pixel facet at each sharp point
         where the raster had rounded the tip. Lucas photographed it: *"er zitten
         allemaal kleine ribbels op het logo."*

         What is here instead: the source upsampled 8x, contoured, reduced with
         Douglas-Peucker to the 15 + 7 vertices the shape actually has, facets
         under 3.5 source px merged away (they are rasterisation, not drawing),
         and the one genuine curve — the shoulder — fitted as a circle and
         emitted as a single A command. 239 bytes, and every straight edge is a
         straight line rather than an approximation of one. Verified by
         rasterising both at source resolution and counting disagreeing pixels:
         0.48% of the ink, spread over 757 blobs of at most 5px — which is the
         anti-aliased edge and nothing else.

         TWO THINGS THAT BIT DURING THAT REBUILD, both caught by diffing the
         render against the source rather than by reading the path. First, a run
         of shallow turns is not necessarily an arc: it is also what a long,
         very slightly bent straight edge looks like, and fitting a circle to
         one produced a 543-unit radius that ballooned the whole inner edge of
         the V into a crescent. Second, the fix for that is not a tighter angle
         threshold but an EDGE-LENGTH rule — a vertex may only join a curve if
         both edges meeting there are short relative to the mark. A shallow turn
         at the end of a 500-unit straight edge is a kink in a straight edge.

         The method is worth repeating for any future mark: upsample, contour,
         Douglas-Peucker, merge sub-pixel facets, fit arcs only where the
         residual proves an arc, and diff the result against the source before
         believing it. A logo made of straight lines should be drawn with
         straight lines.

         THE DRAWING ARRIVED IN TWO TONES and ships in one. A second tone would
         need a second path and a second token in every scope this mark lands
         in, and it buys nothing where it counts: rendered at 16 and 24px the
         lighter blade disappears entirely. The spike reads from the NOTCH, not
         from the tone, so one flat fill loses none of it and keeps the single
         inherited --brand that lets one symbol serve every ground.

         Neither path carries a fill attribute, on purpose. `fill` is an
         inherited SVG property, so every <use> site sets it from a token
         (`.brand-word`/`.brand-mark` read --brand, which flips with the ink
         ramp) and one symbol serves every ground. Do not add fill="currentColor"
         here: it would win over the inherited value and freeze both marks to
         whatever `color` happened to be.

         The marks are never placed together — see the brand lockup guard in
         scripts/brand-lockup-guard.mjs, which fails the build if they are. -->
