          The two marks are alternative signatures and never a lockup (see the
          note by the symbols above, and scripts/brand-lockup-guard.mjs, which
          fails the build if they ever share an ancestor below <body>). Putting
          the glyph here and leaving the logotype in the footer is exactly the
          intended use of that pair: the mark signs the chrome, the word signs
          the page. Their nearest common ancestor is <body>, so the guard is
          satisfied by construction rather than by having been remembered.

          aria-label stays on the <a> AND on the <svg aria-hidden="true">: the link needs an
          accessible name for a screen reader arriving at the nav, and the
          graphic needs one for anyone who lands on it directly. Neither is
          redundant with the other, and neither can be inferred from a path. */}
      <a href={lp('/')} class="brand" aria-label="VISUAILS home">
